A374717	filfix	0	new A005940()	The even bisection when divided by 2 returns the sequence. The odd bisection when transformed by replacing all factors prime(2^k) in a(2*n-1) with prime(2^(k-1)) also returns the sequence (similar to properties of D005940). The sequence is fixed on numbers of the form 2^n or 3*2^n (J029744), since by the definition points at 2^n are already named as such, and if n = 3*2^r then the powers of 2 adjacent to n are 2^r < 2^(r+1) < 3*2^r < 2^(r+2), from which, by the definition we find k = 2^(r+2) - 3*2^r = 2^r(3 - 2) = 2^r, which is a fixed point so a(k) = 2^r, and a(n) is least novel m*a(k); m a term in J033844. Since 2^r is already a term the required m is 3, so a(n) = 3*2^r = n (compare with fixed points of D005940). (End)
